-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses the 2Q policy, which separates sheets used once from sheets used more than once so a one-off screening scan does not displace the comparison working set.
+
+Two queues hold cached entries:
+
+- **First-use queue** — sheets admitted but not yet requested a second time, ordered oldest-first.
+- **Reused queue** — sheets requested again since admission, ordered least-recently-used first.
+
+A completed sheet enters the first-use queue. A cache hit on a sheet in the first-use queue promotes it to the reused queue. Eviction removes the oldest entry from the first-use queue; only when that queue is empty does eviction remove the least-recently-used entry from the reused queue. A long sequential screening therefore churns through the first-use queue while sheets an editor has already revisited survive in the reused queue.
+
+The reused queue holds the larger share of the 12 GiB budget; the split between the two queues is a tunable parameter (see Open Questions). Queue membership and all replacement bookkeeping stay private to the preview worker; no other node reads or configures it.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q — a first-use queue and a reused queue, evicting first-use entries before reused ones | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Workloads mix long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small working set, so no policy is best for all. LRU follows recent work cheaply but a screening scan evicts the comparison set. Aged frequency protects repeatedly popular sheets but adds counter maintenance and aging, and lags a project switch by retaining stale favorites. 2Q resists scans by admitting new sheets to a first-use queue that absorbs the churn and promoting only revisited sheets to the reused queue, at the cost of a second queue and a tunable size split. All three keep the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget unchanged; the choice is internal to the worker. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Cache eviction policy: 2Q, chosen to protect the comparison working set from screening scans (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Initial first-use/reused queue split — to be tuned against observed workloads during the pilot.
+2. Whether to add a ghost queue of recently evicted first-use keys, so a re-request after eviction is recognized as a reuse rather than a first reference. The two-queue form above omits it.
